WEBVTT

1
00:00:14.023 --> 00:00:16.524
I turned around to see who had spoken to us in French.

2
00:00:17.304 --> 00:00:18.464
It was the captain of the vessel!

3
00:00:19.464 --> 00:00:22.685
Ned immediately released the steward, who tottered out of the cabin.

4
00:00:23.645 --> 00:00:28.507
Leaning against the corner of the table, the captain folded his arms and studied us with great care.

5
00:00:29.407 --> 00:00:31.388
Did he regret the words that he had just spoken?

6
00:00:32.268 --> 00:00:32.668
I wondered.

7
00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:36.903
For several moments there was silence, and none of us dared to break it.

8
00:00:36.923 --> 00:00:45.510
"Gentlemen," the captain said in a calm and penetrating voice, "I am fluent in French, English, German, and

9
00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:51.455
Latin. Therefore I could have answered you earlier, but I wanted time to reflect.

10
00:00:52.455 --> 00:00:56.559
Each one of you told essentially the same story, so I believe you were telling the truth.

11
00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:03.592
I now know that you are Professor Pierre Aronnax of the Paris Museum of Natural History, his servant

12
00:01:03.933 --> 00:01:08.415
Conseil, and Ned Land, a Canadian harpooner aboard the U.S.

13
00:01:08.475 --> 00:01:10.016
Navy warship Abraham Lincoln."

14
00:01:11.597 --> 00:01:13.758
I bowed slightly to show that he was correct.

15
00:01:14.658 --> 00:01:19.281
The captain spoke French with perfect ease and without a trace of a foreign accent.

16
00:01:20.001 --> 00:01:21.962
Yet he didn't seem to be from France.

17
00:01:23.023 --> 00:01:27.466
"No doubt you think that I've delayed a long time in paying you this second visit," the captain

18
00:01:27.506 --> 00:01:31.708
continued. "But I needed time to ponder my course of action toward you.

19
00:01:32.072 --> 00:01:36.514
Chance has brought you into the presence of a man who has cut all ties with humanity.

20
00:01:36.894 --> 00:01:39.115
Your arrival has disrupted my whole existence."

21
00:01:40.136 --> 00:01:41.796
"Unintentionally!" I replied.

22
00:01:41.816 --> 00:01:45.878
"Unintentionally?" replied the captain, raising his voice a little.

23
00:01:46.638 --> 00:01:50.540
"Was it unintentionally that the Abraham Lincoln chased me all over the Pacific?

24
00:01:51.441 --> 00:01:54.502
Was it unintentionally that you became a passenger on that warship?

25
00:01:55.402 --> 00:01:58.944
Was it unintentionally that your cannon balls rebounded off my vessel?

26
00:01:59.956 --> 00:02:03.519
Was it unintentionally that Ned Land struck it with his harpoon?"

27
00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:09.543
I detected a restrained irritation in these words but knew that I had to respond.

28
00:02:09.563 --> 00:02:17.109
"Sir, you are probably unaware that everyone in Europe and America is talking about your vessel," I said.

29
00:02:17.129 --> 00:02:24.014
"In pursuing you, those onboard the Abraham Lincoln believed that they were chasing a sea creature, a horrible

30
00:02:24.114 --> 00:02:26.676
monster that they had to destroy at any price."

31
00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:29.922
The captain was not impressed with my explanation.

32
00:02:29.942 --> 00:02:35.767
"I'm sure that your warship would have been just as happy to destroy a submarine," he said.

33
00:02:36.768 --> 00:02:39.610
I'm afraid that I agreed with him, but I didn't say that aloud.

34
00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:44.134
"Therefore," he continued, "I'm within my rights to treat you as enemies.

35
00:02:44.674 --> 00:02:49.578
I could place you on top of this vessel and then sink below the waves, forgetting you ever

36
00:02:49.678 --> 00:02:52.881
existed." "No civilized man would do that!"

37
00:02:53.301 --> 00:02:53.882
I protested.

38
00:02:53.902 --> 00:02:56.524
"I'm not a civilized man.

39
00:02:57.032 --> 00:03:00.774
I have finished with society, for reasons that I alone know.

40
00:03:01.614 --> 00:03:04.835
Therefore I do not obey society's laws."

41
00:03:05.836 --> 00:03:09.997
A flash of anger kindled in his eyes, and I had a glimpse of his terrible past.

42
00:03:10.778 --> 00:03:13.259
After a rather long silence, he went on.

43
00:03:13.279 --> 00:03:18.281
"I've decided that there's a way to reconcile my pity for you with my own interests.

44
00:03:18.721 --> 00:03:21.962
You will remain onboard my vessel since fate has cast you here.

45
00:03:22.323 --> 00:03:28.474
You will be free, but in exchange for this liberty, I must impose one condition and you must

46
00:03:28.574 --> 00:03:29.674
swear to agree to it."

47
00:03:30.775 --> 00:03:31.355
"What is that?"

48
00:03:31.895 --> 00:03:32.276
I asked.

49
00:03:32.296 --> 00:03:38.419
"From time to time, I may have to confine you to your cabins for hours or even days,"

50
00:03:38.879 --> 00:03:39.480
said the captain.

51
00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:43.282
"As I never want to use violence, I expect you to obey.

52
00:03:43.722 --> 00:03:44.743
Do you accept this condition?"

53
00:03:45.803 --> 00:03:47.164
"We do," I replied.

54
00:03:47.184 --> 00:03:50.706
"But what do you mean when you say that we'll be free?"

55
00:03:50.726 --> 00:03:54.248
"You'll be allowed to freely wander the submarine."

56
00:03:55.324 --> 00:03:56.865
It dawned on me what this really meant.

57
00:03:56.885 --> 00:04:00.586
"Do you mean that we'll never see our friends and families again?"

58
00:04:00.606 --> 00:04:02.967
"Yes, sir," replied the captain.

59
00:04:02.987 --> 00:04:05.068
"What!" exclaimed Ned.

60
00:04:05.088 --> 00:04:07.809
"I can't swear that I'll never try to escape."

61
00:04:07.829 --> 00:04:09.850
"But this is cruelty!"

62
00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:10.711
I cried.

63
00:04:10.731 --> 00:04:13.532
"No, it's compassion," said the captain.

64
00:04:13.552 --> 00:04:18.214
"You are my prisoners of war, and I could throw you into the ocean depths if I wanted

65
00:04:18.254 --> 00:04:23.176
to. You attacked me and inadvertently found the secret of my whole existence.

66
00:04:23.532 --> 00:04:26.314
Do you think I'm going to send you back into the world with that secret?

67
00:04:26.734 --> 00:04:31.017
Never! In restraining you, it's not you that I guard but myself."

68
00:04:31.037 --> 00:04:35.560
"So you are basically giving us a choice between life and death?"

69
00:04:36.121 --> 00:04:36.461
I asked.

70
00:04:37.421 --> 00:04:42.645
"Yes." But then he went on in a gentler tone, "I know your work, Professor Aronnax.

71
00:04:44.186 --> 00:04:47.348
Your book on the mysteries of the seas is one of my favorites.

72
00:04:48.429 --> 00:04:52.692
You've done as good a job as you could from land, but you haven't seen nearly enough.

73
00:04:53.632 --> 00:04:59.176
You won't regret your time onboard my vessel because I'm about to embark on an underwater tour of

74
00:04:59.216 --> 00:04:59.737
the world."

75
00:05:00.758 --> 00:05:05.121
I have to admit that this intrigued me, and for a moment I forgot that we were prisoners.

76
00:05:05.141 --> 00:05:07.683
"By what name should we address you?"

77
00:05:08.183 --> 00:05:08.544
I asked.

78
00:05:08.564 --> 00:05:16.190
"To you, I'm just Captain Nemo, and to me, you're just passengers on my submarine, the Nautilus."

79
00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:19.092
I smiled when the captain gave his name.

80
00:05:20.112 --> 00:05:25.416
Obviously he was intent on keeping his identity secret because nemo means "nobody" in Latin.

81
00:05:26.377 --> 00:05:28.238
Captain Nemo called and a steward appeared.

82
00:05:29.099 --> 00:05:33.122
The captain gave the steward his orders in that strange language that I couldn't understand.

83
00:05:34.583 --> 00:05:36.704
Then he turned to Ned and Conseil.

84
00:05:36.724 --> 00:05:41.428
"Follow the steward to your cabin, where breakfast is waiting," said Captain Nemo.

85
00:05:41.448 --> 00:05:45.871
"And now, Professor Aronnax, our meal is also ready.

86
00:05:46.251 --> 00:05:47.312
Permit me to show you the way."

87
00:05:48.352 --> 00:05:51.335
He led me through a passage until we came to an elegant dining room.

88
00:05:52.295 --> 00:05:58.060
Tall oak cabinets stood at both ends of the room; their shelves were filled with china and glass

89
00:05:58.100 --> 00:05:59.501
of inestimable value.

90
00:06:00.462 --> 00:06:03.704
In the center of the room was a table spread with a fine meal.

91
00:06:04.425 --> 00:06:06.286
Captain Nemo indicated where I should sit.

92
00:06:07.207 --> 00:06:10.169
I recognized some of the foods at this breakfast but not all of them.

93
00:06:11.030 --> 00:06:12.792
Captain Nemo seemed to read my thoughts.

94
00:06:13.792 --> 00:06:18.520
"Most of these dishes are unknown to you," he said, "but they all come from the sea and

95
00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:19.861
you may eat them without fear.

96
00:06:20.401 --> 00:06:24.844
For a long time my crew and I have renounced food from the land, and we are healthy."

97
00:06:24.864 --> 00:06:27.225
"Then where did you get this meat?"

98
00:06:27.645 --> 00:06:28.786
I pointed to one of the dishes.

99
00:06:28.806 --> 00:06:33.649
"That which you believe to be meat, Professor, is really fillet of turtle.

100
00:06:34.009 --> 00:06:37.831
My chef is a clever fellow who excels at cooking the various products of the sea."

101
00:06:38.871 --> 00:06:42.273
I ate while Captain Nemo entertained me with extraordinary stories.

102
00:06:42.293 --> 00:06:44.394
"You like the sea, Captain?"

103
00:06:44.815 --> 00:06:45.175
I asked.

104
00:06:46.135 --> 00:06:46.856
"Yes, I love it!

105
00:06:47.296 --> 00:06:48.737
The sea doesn't belong to tyrants.

106
00:06:49.197 --> 00:06:54.680
Upon its surface men can still wage war, but at thirty feet below the surface, their power disappears.

107
00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:58.482
Here I recognize no masters and I am free!"

108
00:06:59.483 --> 00:07:03.345
Captain Nemo suddenly fell silent in the midst of his enthusiastic outpouring.

109
00:07:04.025 --> 00:07:05.726
Did he feel that he had said too much?

110
00:07:06.706 --> 00:07:09.788
For a few moments he paced up and down, much agitated.

111
00:07:10.729 --> 00:07:15.131
Then he grew calmer and his face recovered its usual icy composure.

112
00:07:15.151 --> 00:07:21.489
"Now, Professor," he said, turning to me, "if you'd like to inspect the Nautilus, come this way."
